# THE HOLD WALK: every `hold` of a program checked, each task's body walked in order, threading what is known. A
# branch condition refines its branch and its negation the next, a binding or assignment contributes its defining
# equality, every write first forgets what it changes, a loop forgets before its body what a later turn changes, and
# what follows a branch is what every path into it shows (check/hold-facts.tree). Each hold's goal is then decided from
# what is known there (check/inequality.tree, check/universal.tree), a theorem's after its citations' conclusions are
# added, and an unproven one is reported, a tier-0 obligation counted instead.
#
#   - A TASK WHOSE EVERY HOLD WAS PROVEN BEFORE, ASKED THE SAME QUESTION, IS PROVEN AGAIN: the walk's key is the task as
#     checked without its places and every whole-program fact about a name it mentions, and only a walk that refused
#     nothing is kept, as the counts it added
#   - a rule that did not hold says what it was asked, and, when small values of its marks satisfy every hypothesis and
#     break the goal, that it is false there (check/explain.tree)
#
# Ported from check/holds.ts (self-hosting, 2026-10-06), part four. The decisions:
#   - what the face still holds is handed in as `walk-calls`: the proof budget of check/product.ts, the width switch,
#     the counter of uncertified answers, the replay counter the tests read, and the profile a run may ask for
#   - the program's sets the caller already knows (pure, state-free, length-keeping, fresh-returning, the functions) are
#     the face's, cached per program by check/facts.ts, and arrive as hashes
#   - a memo key is the original's JSON text, written part by part through binds, and hashed by term/hash.tree

